Why this licence
----------------

It is not a preference; it is the only choice that fits what the app already
contains. Vessel vendors the Winlator X server (LGPL-2.1) directly into the APK,
so the app's own terms have to be LGPL-compatible.

"or later" is kept because it costs nothing and leaves the door open to a future
GPL-3-family component. This paragraph used to give a different and load-bearing
reason: that libadrenotools is under version 3 of the LGPL and that only the "or
any later version" clause permits combining with it. That was wrong.
libadrenotools is BSD-2-Clause, checked at the vendored commit, and constrains
nothing here. The correction is recorded in docs/LICENSING.md rather than quietly
deleted, because a reason that turns out to be false is worth a note.

GPL-3.0 would also have been compatible and was rejected as stricter than the
obligation requires: it would impose full copyleft on the whole application
where the LGPL only asks that the LGPL parts stay free and relinkable.


What this means in practice
---------------------------

Section 6 asks for three things when a work containing the Library is
distributed, and all three are about the APK rather than about this repository:

  1. Prominent notice, with each copy of the work, that the Library is used in
     it and is covered by this License.
  2. A copy of this License, supplied with the work. The full text ships inside
     the APK, at res/raw/license_lgpl_2_1.txt.
  3. One of subsections 6(a) to 6(e). Vessel relies on 6(a): the complete
     corresponding source of both the Library and the work that uses it is in
     this repository, unobfuscated and buildable, with the upstream commit and
     every local change recorded in app/src/main/java/com/winlator/README.md, so
     a user can modify the Library and rebuild an APK containing their version.
     R8 keep rules in app/proguard-rules.pro stop minification renaming those
     packages; if it ever starts touching them, that is a licensing regression
     rather than a build one.

6(a) holds only while this repository is actually public, and item 1 needs a
place in the interface that says so. docs/LICENSING.md tracks both.

Third-party components are not covered by this file. Each keeps its own licence
and its own copyright headers; see docs/LICENSING.md for the full picture,
including the components shipped as separate .wcp packages rather than compiled
into the app.
